WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:08.590
You must have seen a variety of magnets around
you.

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:12.330
A Bar magnet and a
horseshoe magnet are something that most of

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.350
us know about.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:18.840
Apart from these
two, a cylindrical shaped magnet, a ring-shaped

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.230
magnet, and a spherical
magnet are a few more types!

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:25.940
But these are not the different TYPES OF MAGNETS.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.710
These are actually
different SHAPES of magnets.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:32.759
So how do we classify magnets into different
types?

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:36.580
One of the ways in which we can classify them
is based on their occurrence.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:41.370
That is whether they are naturally occurring
magnets… or maybe artificial or

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.530
manmade magnets.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:47.640
Almost all the magnets that you will ever
come across including the ones we

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.820
saw are artificial.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:52.790
So what are natural magnets then?

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:57.030
Lodestone is the strongest naturally occurring
magnet.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:02.470
It is black in color and
was the first magnet ever discovered!

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:06.670
Some other naturally occurring magnetic materials
are pyrrhotite, columbite

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:10.270
and even basalt has some magnetic properties!

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.960
But these magnets are much
weaker than lodestone.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.360
There is one more way in which magnets are
classified.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:20.990
This one simply
depends on how long they remain as magnets!

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:26.190
Yes, the magnets are also
classified into two main categories: permanent

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:27.190
and temporary.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:32.530
From their names, you should be able to tell
me their logical definitions!

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:36.530
You would probably say that permanent magnets
remain as magnets forever

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:39.160
or for a very long time!

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.880
And temporary magnets remain as magnets for
a very

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.640
short period of time!

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.670
These are okayish definitions but not proper.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:49.890
So what are the proper ones
then?

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.489
Okay, here we go!

00:01:52.489 --> 00:01:56.569
The magnets that retain their magnetism in
the absence of an external

00:01:56.569 --> 00:01:59.830
magnetic field are called permanent magnets.

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:03.229
Their magnetic strength never
really fades on its own!

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:07.850
And the magnets that cannot retain their magnetism
or retain it for a very

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:11.810
short time are called temporary magnets.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.810
Temporary magnets act as magnets
only while they’re within a strong magnetic

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:16.810
field!

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:22.380
Don’t worry we will understand these definitions
in detail in the next video!

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.620
But before that, let’s quickly review what
we learned!

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:30.960
Based on their occurrence, magnets are classified
into naturally occurring and

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.060
artificial magnets!

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.841
And based on how long they retain their magnetism,
they

00:02:35.841 --> 00:02:39.920
are classified into permanent and temporary
magnets!

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.430
And there’s one
important relation here that we should know

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.000
about!

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.110
All the naturally occurring magnets are permanent!

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:48.390
Yes!

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:52.370
We will continue
understanding more concepts in the coming

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:56.099
videos!

